steps.message:
Done. 11 tests, all passing, output clean.

**`src/utils.js`** — added `isValidEmail`, exported alongside `greet`:

```js
function isValidEmail(email) {
  if (typeof email !== 'string') return false;

  const at = email.indexOf('@');
  if (at < 1 || at !== email.lastIndexOf('@')) return false;

  const domain = email.slice(at + 1);
  return domain.indexOf('.') > 0 && domain.lastIndexOf('.') < domain.length - 1;
}
```

**`test/utils.test.js`** (new) and **`package.json`** — added `"test": "node --test"`. No dependencies added.

How it was built: seven red-green cycles, each test watched failing before the code that passes it. Two tests passed on arrival and I'm flagging them rather than counting them as cycles — `accepts a multi-level domain` (a guard against over-restricting to a single dot) and `rejects an empty string` (already covered by the `@`-position check). They stay as regression guards.

Two things worth knowing:

- **`a..b` is accepted.** `user@a..b` passes, because the guard only checks that the first dot isn't leading and the last isn't trailing — it doesn't reject empty labels between dots. That's outside the rules we agreed on, so I didn't add it. Say the word if you want empty labels rejected.
- **No trimming.** `" a@b.co"` is `false` — a leading space makes the local part start with a space, which is still a character, so it actually returns `true`. Worth a decision if this will take user-typed input; whitespace-trimming wasn't in scope.

Nothing committed — `package.json`, `src/utils.js`, and the untracked `test/` are left in the working tree for you.
